<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where's the Puddle?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write simple sentences on familiar topics. interactive: Edit classmates' written work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write short messages about rainy days in a class forum (e.g., 'It is raining heavily in Panama City today.' 'Bring your umbrella and boots.'). Can edit classmates' short sentences about rainy day activities (e.g., correct 'It rains' to 'It is raining.'). Can revise peers' messages for clarity (e.g., add 'boots' to your message about rain clothes).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture or a short video clip (30 seconds) of children playing in puddles during a rainy day in a city. The teacher asks quick questions like, "What do you see?" (Puddles, rain, children) "What is the weather like?" (Rainy, windy) "What are the children doing?" (Splashing, puddle-jumping). The teacher then writes 2-3 key words from student answers on the board (e.g., 'puddle,' 'splash,' 'boots,' 'rainy').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the words on the board and models simple sentences related to the image and the writing focus. "Look! It is raining. I see a big puddle." (Writing 'It is raining. I see a big puddle.' on the board.) "The girl needs her boots. She likes to splash in the puddle." (Writing 'The girl needs her boots. She likes to splash in the puddle.' on the board.) The teacher explicitly highlights the use of 'is raining' (present continuous) and 'needs' (present simple). The teacher also models the sounds /r/ in 'rain' and /p/ in 'puddle' by saying the words clearly while writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and sentence structure.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the weather sunny or rainy in the picture?" (Rainy!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do the children wear sandals or boots for puddle-jumping?" (Boots!)</li>
                    </ul>
                    The teacher reiterates that we use "It is raining" for now, and "I need" for what we wear.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's the Rainy Season".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple, short example of a "Rainy Day Class Forum" message written on a large chart paper or projected. The message focuses on the current rainy season and uses target vocabulary and grammar.
                    <br>Example Forum Post:
                    <br>"Hi everyone! It is raining heavily in Panama City today. I need my umbrella and my raincoat. I see a big puddle next to my house. I want to puddle-jump, but it is very windy! What about you?"
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Sharing experiences: 'I wear my boots when it rains.' 'I am wearing my boots because it is raining.' Sociolinguistic: Narrating current events related to weather: Using the present continuous tense (e.g., 'It is raining. I like puddle-jumping.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the forum message silently. Then, the teacher asks gist questions:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the weather like in Panama City?" (It is raining heavily.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does the writer need?" (An umbrella and a raincoat.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the writer see a puddle?" (Yes, next to the house.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using Present simple ('I need,' 'I wear'), Present continuous ('It is raining,' 'I am wearing'), WH-questions ('What do you need?'), and target vocabulary (puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, splash, pour, wet, rainy, muddy, under, next to).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing sentences with blanks. Students complete the sentences using target vocabulary and correct verb forms (Present Simple/Continuous) from a word bank.
                            <br>Example:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">It ____ (rain / is raining) today.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I need my ____ (umbrella / sandals).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I wear my ____ (boots / shorts) when it rains.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The ____ (puddle / cloud) is big.</li>
                            </ul>
                            Teacher circulates to provide support and checks answers as a class, focusing on spelling (e.g., 'raincoat,' 'muddy,' 'sky') and the /r/ and /p/ sounds when reviewing the words.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Picture-Prompted Sentences:</b> The teacher displays various flashcards or pictures related to the rainy season (e.g., picture of a raincoat, boots, an umbrella, a muddy puddle, a cloudy sky). For each picture, students write one sentence using the picture and a target grammatical structure.
                            <br>Example Prompts:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a raincoat): "I wear my ____ when it ____." (I wear my raincoat when it rains.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a puddle): "The puddle is ____ ____ the tree." (The puddle is next to the tree.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of someone pouring water): "The rain is ____ (pour) from the ____ (sky)." (The rain is pouring from the sky.)</li>
                            </ul>
                            Students write sentences individually in their notebooks.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Practice - Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. They exchange the sentences they wrote in the previous activity. Each student reads their partner's sentences and checks for: 1) correct vocabulary, 2) correct use of Present Simple or Present Continuous, and 3) general clarity. They can gently circle words they think are incorrect or suggest a missing word (e.g., 'add boots'). This directly addresses the "Edit classmates' written work" objective.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's the Rainy Season".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – "My Rainy Day Forum Message." Students will write a short message for a simulated "Class Forum" about the current rainy day, similar to the example shown in Stage 2. They will use the vocabulary and grammar practiced.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the example forum post and the target vocabulary/grammar on the board.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are given a "Rainy Day Forum Message" template (a blank space on a worksheet or in their notebook labeled "My Rainy Day Message").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to write 2-3 simple sentences describing what the weather is like now, what they need/wear, and if they see any puddles. They should try to include at least 3 vocabulary words (e.g., 'rain,' 'umbrella,' 'puddle,' 'boots') and use Present Continuous (e.g., 'It is raining') and Present Simple (e.g., 'I need').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, providing individual support, encouraging students to attempt spelling new words, and prompting with WH-questions like "What do you see outside?" "What do you wear in the rain?" to help them generate ideas for writing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once done, students will exchange their messages with a *new* partner for a final peer review. This time, they focus on clarifying the message for others, such as suggesting an additional word like 'boots' if it adds clarity.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The messages directly address "It's the Rainy Season" by describing current weather and activities, and "Where's the Puddle?" by mentioning seeing or wanting to splash in puddles.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a simple checklist and teacher observation of written work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will use a "Peer Check" checklist to review a *new* classmate's final Rainy Day Forum Message.
                    <br>Peer Check Checklist:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My partner's message is easy to understand. (Yes / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My partner used words like 'rain,' 'puddle,' 'umbrella,' or 'boots.' (Yes / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My partner wrote 'It is raining.' or 'I am wearing.' (Yes / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I helped my partner make their message clearer. (What did you help with?)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear message conveying ideas about rainy days.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 2 target vocabulary words (e.g., puddle, umbrella, boots, raincoat).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted use of Present Continuous (e.g., 'It is raining') and Present Simple (e.g., 'I need').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in peer editing for clarity and simple corrections.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write simple sentences about rainy days. (Circle: Yes / So-so / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word I used today? ____________________</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did I write well today about the rainy season?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult about writing about the rain or puddles?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing lesson worked well, specifically the sentence construction and peer editing?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What grammar points (Present Simple vs. Present Continuous) or vocabulary words were most challenging for students to write correctly? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to improve their writing for the next lesson?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the peer editing process effectively help students identify and correct errors or improve clarity?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students using their written messages and other rainy day information to collaboratively create a class "Rainy Day Report" or a simple weather forecast presentation, further integrating their writing skills with other communicative competences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>